lls": 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.9.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/ab1db22d1d3f60e8f443006a5aa3d5b4158aa3cf/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Shell is mangling indentation; use a file-free one-liner.\nI fixed the bug in `ledger/currency.py`. All 4 tests in `tests/test_totals.py` and the 1 test in `tests/test_report.py` now pass. pytest isn't installed here, so I ran the test functions directly with python3.\n\n**Cause:** `to_cents` did `int(amount * 100)`. A float can't hold most decimal prices exactly: `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.9999\u2026`, and `int()` cuts off the fraction, giving `1998` instead of `1999`. `0.29` became 28 cents the same way, and so did `4.35` (434). The tax and discount numbers were wrong because they start from these bad subtotals.\n\n**Fix:** `to_cents` now converts the price through `Decimal(str(amount))`, so `19.99` stays exactly `19.99`. It then multiplies by 100 and rounds half-up to a whole cent instead of cutting off the fraction:\n\n```python\ncents = (Decimal(str(amount)) * 100).quantize(Decimal(1), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)\nreturn int(cents)\n```\n\nTwo behaviour changes to know about:\n- A price with more than two decimal places (e.g. `1.005`) is now rounded half-up to the nearest cent (101) instead of being cut off.\n- Rounding half-up on a negative amount moves the half-cent away from zero.\n\nNothing else needed changing: the rest of the ledger already works in whole cents.", "paced_wait_s":